{"body":"## Summary\n\n`resolve-impact.py`'s heuristic matches the **basename and stem of each changed path as a substring of every document body**. In a repository whose documentation deliberately cites file paths, that saturates: in this repo it selected **89 of 97 corpus documents**. Worse, it is in *direct structural conflict* with the remediation doctrine the audit itself pushes you toward — \"don't duplicate a value, point at the file that owns it\" — because every such pointer adds another file-path mention and therefore another heuristic match. The better the documentation gets, the less selective the impact map becomes.\n\n## Evidence (akira993/akiras-portfolio, engine 0.12.0)\n\n`docGlobs` corpus = 97 documents. `minIdentifierLength` = 5 (default). Measured with the engine's own token rule:\n\n| window | changed paths | tokens | heuristic-matched docs |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\n| stale anchor `07239a3f..HEAD` | 169 | 315 | **89 / 97** |\n| single commits (32 sampled from history) | 1–47 | — | median **18**, min 4, max 70 |\n\nTuning the documented escape hatch does essentially nothing:\n\n- adding the 30+ most frequent generic basenames to `heuristics.excludeBasenames`: **89 → 87**.\n- The distribution is long-tailed rather than dominated by a few bad tokens: the top token (`emdash`) matches 37 docs, `wrangler` 31, `DESIGN` 22, then a long tail.\n\nRestricting tokens to code-only changed paths (dropping tokens derived from changed `docs/**` and `.claude/**`) gives **81** — still saturated.\n\n## The doctrine conflict, concretely\n\nSix rounds of remediation in this repo followed the rule \"replace a duplicated value with a pointer to the file that owns it\" (it is the single most effective fix pattern we found). Each such fix adds a backticked path to a document. Those paths are exactly what the heuristic substring-matches. So a repository that has been remediated *well* is one where the heuristic selects nearly everything.\n\n## Suggestions\n\n1. **Report the coverage ratio and warn on saturation.** `counts.heuristicOnly / len(docGlobs corpus)` is already computable in `resolve-impact.py`. Emitting a non-blocking warning above some threshold (say 50%) with the message \"impactMap is not carrying the selection; consider adding rules\" would have told us in round 1 what took six rounds to measure.\n2. **Make `impactMap` the primary and heuristic the residual, explicitly.** The cap priority (`mapped ≥ heuristic ≥ graphify ≥ semantic`) already implements this; what is missing is guidance that a healthy configuration has most of its selection in `mapped`. In this repo enriching impactMap from 17 to 31 rules moved `mapped` 16 → 39 and `heuristicOnly` 75 → 53, with the impacted total unchanged at 92 — i.e. precision improved but the heuristic's saturation hid it in the totals.\n3. **Consider excluding tokens derived from changed documentation paths** (or making that opt-in). Doc→doc impact is real, but `impactMap` is the right place to express it; a changed `DESIGN.md` producing the token `DESIGN` and matching 22 documents is noise.\n4. **Note in the docs that the dominant cost driver is anchor staleness**, not `maxImpactedDocs`. Our per-run cost was ~3.6M tokens at 92 docs; the median single-commit window would have been ~18 docs.\n","number":40,"title":"resolve-impact's token heuristic saturates (89/97 docs) and is in structural conflict with the pointer-to-owner remediation doctrine","url":"https://github.com/akira993/doc-audit-harness/issues/40"}
{"body":"## Summary\n\nPer §Phase 4 step 3d, a codex review that fails to launch, times out, or returns an unparseable result is a **non-blocking WARN** with `CODEX_REVIEW_STATE=execution-failed` and no findings folded. That is a reasonable default in general — but in a repository where the codex layer is the one producing the blocking findings, it means a run can return **CONSISTENT with the only effective layer silently absent**, and the anchor then advances.\n\n## How we nearly hit it\n\nThree consecutive rounds here had their blocking finding come from codex alone (two of them with Phase 3 at zero FAILs — see companion issue). In the same period, editing our own `.envrc` caused `direnv` to revoke approval; our codex invocation was `direnv exec . codex exec ...`, so it began failing with:\n\n```\ndirenv: error /path/.envrc is blocked. Run `direnv allow` to approve its content\n```\n\nexit code 0 from the wrapper, no result file. Had that happened during Phase 4 rather than during a pre-commit review, the run would have recorded a WARN, folded zero findings, and — with Phase 3 clean that round — returned CONSISTENT and advanced the anchor. Nothing in the report would have distinguished that from a genuinely clean audit except one status line among fourteen.\n\nThe trigger is mundane and likely to recur: any change to a file that a wrapper (`direnv`, `mise`, `asdf`, a devShell) gates on will silently disarm the layer.\n\n## Suggestions\n\n1. **Make the degrade visible in the verdict line, not only in the tool-status list.** If `CODEX_REVIEW_STATE != completed` and the verdict is CONSISTENT, say so next to the verdict: \"CONSISTENT (codex-review did not run: `<state>`)\".\n2. **Offer a strict mode.** `codexReview.required: true` → an `execution-failed` state REFUSES rather than warns. Repos that have come to depend on this layer could opt in.\n3. **Pre-flight the invocation.** The probe currently runs `codex --version` at Phase 0 through the plain binary, but the actual Phase-4 invocation may go through a wrapper. Probing with the *same* invocation shape would have caught the direnv block before sealing.\n4. Minor: the current probe passing while the real invocation fails is itself worth a note in the skill text — the two are not the same command.\n","number":42,"title":"A failed codex-review is a non-blocking WARN, so a run can return CONSISTENT with the only effective layer silently absent","url":"https://github.com/akira993/doc-audit-harness/issues/42"}
{"body":"## 背景\n\ndir-framework 系プロジェクトは、コミット前ゲート用の決定論マップを持つようになった（akira993/dir-framework@5ff26a9・REQ-002）:\n\n- `.claude/audit-scope.json` — 変更ファイル glob → 確認候補文書のマップ。`impact: none` は恒久的に文書非関連な分類のみ。空影響先・重複 glob・裸 catch-all は `--validate` が拒否\n- `scripts/resolve-audit-scope.py` — HEAD 差分（staged+unstaged）∪ 未追跡を NUL 区切りで解析し、確認候補文書を決定論的に出力。UNMAPPED ≥1 は exit 2 とし、貼り付け可能な規則スニペットを生成\n- 運用: AGENTS.md ルール16「監査対象はこの解決結果を正とし、エージェント裁量で広げ・狭めしない。UNMAPPED はマップへ規則追加後に通す」\n\n一方 docaudit は `doc-audit.json` の `impactMap` で同じ「変更→影響文書」の対応を持つ。**同一プロジェクト内で2つの決定論マップが並立し、二重管理になる**（片方だけ規則追加され乖離する未来が確実）。\n\n## 提案\n\nimpactMap と audit-scope.json の単一 owner 化。方向は2案:\n\n1. docaudit がプロジェクトの `.claude/audit-scope.json` を読む（impactMap を deprecate、または audit-scope を impactMap のソースとして取り込む）\n2. `docaudit:init` / `--harness --refresh` が audit-scope.json から impactMap を生成する（audit-scope が正本）\n\nいずれの場合も、resolver 側の実装知見（UNMAPPED の fail-closed＋スニペット自動生成が保守コストを大きく下げる）は impactMap 側にも有効。\n\n## 注記: シード自身への docaudit 未導入\n\ndir-framework の docs/runbooks/phase-template.md は `/docaudit:audit` を Phase 完了条件で必須とするが、シード自身は doc-audit.json 未導入で乖離している。統合方針が決まった後、シードへの導入（または phase-template 側の条件分岐）を検討する。\n","number":44,"title":"プロジェクト側 audit-scope.json と impactMap の統合（単一 owner 化）","url":"https://github.com/akira993/doc-audit-harness/issues/44"}

